+# Go HTTP Proxy
+
+A simple HTTP/HTTPS proxy server and client wrapper written in Go.
+
+## Features
+
+- HTTP and HTTPS (CONNECT tunneling) support
+- Minimal request logging
+- Command wrapper client for easy proxy usage
+- No admin privileges required for client
+- Configurable port (defaults to 3128)
+- **Automatic Node.js support** - works with apps that ignore proxy env vars
+
+## Building
+
+### Using Make (recommended)
+
+```bash
+make all
+```
+
+This will create two binaries:
+- `go_proxy_server` - the proxy server
+- `go_proxy` - the client wrapper
+
+### Manual build
+
+```bash
+go build -o go_proxy_server ./cmd/server
+go build -o go_proxy ./cmd/client
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+### 1. Start the proxy server
+
+```bash
+./go_proxy_server
+```
+
+The server will start listening on port 3128 and log all requests:
+
+```
+Starting HTTP proxy server on :3128
+[2025-11-03 10:30:45] CONNECT example.com:443
+[2025-11-03 10:30:46] GET example.com
+```
+
+Stop the server with Ctrl+C for graceful shutdown.
+
+### 2. Use the client wrapper
+
+The client wrapper runs commands with HTTP/HTTPS proxy environment variables automatically set:
+
+```bash
+./go_proxy <command> [args...]
+```
+
+#### Examples
+
+Test with curl:
+```bash
+./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+./go_proxy curl -I https://api.github.com
+```
+
+Use with any command that respects proxy environment variables:
+```bash
+./go_proxy wget https://example.com
+./go_proxy git clone https://github.com/user/repo.git
+./go_proxy npm install
+```
+
+Use with the Claude CLI (as mentioned in your example):
+```bash
+./go_proxy claude ...args
+```
+
+### Manual proxy configuration
+
+If you prefer to set environment variables manually instead of using the wrapper:
+
+```bash
+export HTTP_PROXY=http://localhost:3128
+export HTTPS_PROXY=http://localhost:3128
+curl https://example.com
+```
+
+### Node.js Applications
+
+**The client automatically supports Node.js apps that don't honor proxy environment variables!**
+
+The client embeds a proxy injection script that is automatically activated for all Node.js processes via `NODE_OPTIONS`. This means Node.js applications will work seamlessly without any configuration:
+
+```bash
+# Works with npm-installed CLI tools
+./go_proxy eslint myfile.js
+./go_proxy webpack build
+./go_proxy typescript tsc
+
+# Works with npm/npx
+./go_proxy npm install
+./go_proxy npx create-react-app my-app
+
+# Works with node scripts
+./go_proxy node my-script.js
+```
+
+**How it works:**
+1. The client extracts an embedded `proxy-inject.js` to a temp file
+2. Sets `NODE_OPTIONS="-r /tmp/go_proxy_inject.js"` automatically
+3. When Node.js starts, it loads the injection script first
+4. The script monkey-patches `http` and `https` modules to use the proxy
+5. Your app runs normally with all HTTP/HTTPS requests proxied
+
+**No configuration needed!** This works with:
+- ✅ npm-installed binaries (eslint, webpack, etc.)
+- ✅ npx commands
+- ✅ node scripts
+- ✅ Any process that runs Node.js
+
+**Optional: Enhanced compatibility**
+
+For best results with some libraries, install `http-proxy-agent`:
+```bash
+npm install -g http-proxy-agent
+```
+
+This is optional - the proxy will work without it, but some libraries that use custom HTTP clients may benefit from it.
+
+**Debug logging:**
+
+To see proxy injection debug output:
+```bash
+NODE_DEBUG=proxy ./go_proxy node my-script.js
+```
+
+## Configuration
+
+### Server Configuration
+
+**Custom Port:**
+
+The server can listen on a custom port via the `PROXY_PORT` environment variable:
+
+```bash
+PROXY_PORT=8080 ./go_proxy_server
+```
+
+**Default:** Port 3128
+
+### Client Configuration
+
+The client can connect to a proxy on a different host/port:
+
+**Environment Variables:**
+- `PROXY_HOST` - Proxy server hostname/IP (default: localhost)
+- `PROXY_PORT` - Proxy server port (default: 3128)
+
+**Examples:**
+
+Connect to local proxy on custom port:
+```bash
+PROXY_PORT=8080 ./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+```
+
+Connect to remote proxy:
+```bash
+PROXY_HOST=proxy.example.com ./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+```
+
+Connect to remote proxy with custom port:
+```bash
+PROXY_HOST=192.168.1.100 PROXY_PORT=8080 ./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+```
+
+Or set globally:
+```bash
+export PROXY_HOST=proxy.example.com
+export PROXY_PORT=8080
+./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+```
+
+### Proxy Chaining
+
+The server will automatically chain through another proxy if it's run with `HTTP_PROXY` or `HTTPS_PROXY` environment variables set:
+
+```bash
+# Start server that chains through another proxy
+HTTP_PROXY=http://upstream-proxy:3128 ./go_proxy_server
+
+# Client requests go through your proxy, which forwards through the upstream proxy
+./go_proxy curl https://example.com
+```
+
+This creates a proxy chain: `client → go_proxy_server → upstream-proxy → destination`
+
+## Project Structure
+
+```
+.
+├── cmd/
+│   ├── server/
+│   │   └── main.go         # Proxy server implementation
+│   └── client/
+│       ├── main.go         # Client wrapper implementation
+│       └── proxy-inject.js # Node.js proxy injection (embedded in binary)
+├── go.mod                  # Go module file
+├── Makefile                # Build automation
+└── README.md               # This file
+```
+
+## How It Works
+
+### Server
+The server handles two types of requests:
+
+1. **HTTP requests**: Forwards the request to the destination server and returns the response
+2. **HTTPS requests (CONNECT)**: Establishes a TCP tunnel between the client and destination server for end-to-end encryption
+
+### Client
+The client wrapper:
+
+1. Takes a command and its arguments
+2. Sets `HTTP_PROXY` and `HTTPS_PROXY` environment variables (for apps that honor them)
+3. Extracts embedded `proxy-inject.js` and sets `NODE_OPTIONS` (for Node.js apps)
+4. Executes the command with the modified environment
+5. Passes through stdin/stdout/stderr and preserves the exit code
+
+This dual approach ensures compatibility with:
+- Applications that respect standard proxy environment variables (curl, wget, git, etc.)
+- Node.js applications that ignore proxy environment variables
+
+## Limitations
+
+- No authentication support
+- No caching
+- Basic logging only (timestamp, method, host)
+- No filtering or access control
+- Not suitable for production use (educational/development purposes)
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Go 1.21 or later
+- No admin/root privileges needed (uses port 3128 which doesn't require privileges)
+
+## Cleaning Up
+
+Remove built binaries:
+```bash
+make clean
+```
+
+## License
+
+This is a simple educational project. Use freely.
